      "side": 1,
      "message": "Thank you for your review：\n\nIn the process of developing the scenario scheduler using the sched_ext framework, we have achieved significant benefits in the market by integrating the tasks of the rt_sched_class into the sched_ext control through the original hook (android_vh_task_should_scx). In a mobile environment, we leveraged the global dispatching characteristics of sched_ext to significantly reduce the original preemption of real-time (RT) tasks over CFS while ensuring RT task scheduling priority and latency. In high-load composite scenarios, the overall machine context switches reduced by nearly 50%, with kernel instruction count decreasing by approximately 30%.\n\nHowever, the original hook still has some shortcomings: for certain specific tasks, such as \u0027scx_ops_helper\u0027 and ‘migration/x’, we cannot change their scheduling class to ext_sched_class. Furthermore, the original hook and __setscheduler_class do not pass the task_struct information; thus, we cannot distinguish these specific tasks based solely on policy and priority.\n\nTherefore, we have devised two solutions:\nSolution 1 (this patch): Add parameters, struct task_struct *p, to __setscheduler_class and android_vh_task_should_scx. Considering that other OEM vendors may have already used the original hook, the original hook will not be modified.\n\nSolution 2 (3871719): Since directly adding parameters to __setscheduler_class may not be allowed, Solution 2 utilizes android_vh_setscheduler_class to reconsider next_sched_class after __setscheduler_class.\n\nCould you offer some advice and see which one is more suitable, Solution 1 or Solution 2?",
